[Assistant]: For a Pareto law on $[x_{m},\infty )$ with shape $\alpha >1$

\begin{equation*}
F(x)=1-\Bigl(\frac{x_{m}}{x}\Bigr)^{\!\alpha}\qquad x\ge x_{m},
\qquad 
{\mathbb P}\{M_{n}>x\}=1-F(x)^{\,n}\;, 
\end{equation*}
so that with $x_{m}=1$, $\alpha =2$,

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}M_{n}
   =\int_{1}^{\infty}\Bigl[\,1-\Bigl(1-x^{-2}\Bigr)^{n}\Bigr]dx ,
\qquad n\ge 1 .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}M_{N}
   =\int_{1}^{\infty}\Bigl[1-
      {\mathbb E}\bigl[(1-x^{-2})^{N}\bigr]\Bigr]dx ,
\end{equation*}
the expectation being taken over the number $N$ of years which is
monitored.  For the logarithmic series

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb P}\{N=k\}=c\,\frac{p^{k}}{k},\qquad 
c^{-1}=-\log(1-p),\qquad k\ge1,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}\!\left[t^{N}\right]
   =c\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{(pt)^{k}}{k}
   =-c\log\!\bigl(1-pt\bigr),\qquad |pt|<1 .
\end{equation*}
With $p=1/2$ we have $c=1/\log 2$ and with $t=1-x^{-2}$,

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}\!\left[(1-x^{-2})^{N}\right]
   =-\frac{1}{\log 2}
     \log\!\Bigl[1-\frac12\bigl(1-x^{-2}\bigr)\Bigr]
   =-\frac{1}{\log 2}
     \log\frac{1+x^{-2}}{2}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the usual normalisation $x_{m}=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}M_{N}
   =\int_{1}^{\infty}
     \Bigl[\,1+\frac{1}{\log 2}
          \log\frac{1+x^{-2}}{2}\Bigr]dx .
\end{equation*}
Putting $t=x^{-2}$, $dx=-\tfrac12t^{-3/2}dt$, and integrating by parts,

\begin{align*}
{\mathbb E}M_{N}
&=
\Bigl[-u(t)t^{-1/2}\Bigr]_{0}^{1}
+\frac1{\log2}\int_{0}^{1}\frac{t^{-1/2}}{1+t}dt,
\qquad 
u(t)=1+\frac1{\log2}\log\frac{1+t}{2},
\end{align*}
and $u(1)=1$, $\lim_{t\to0^{+}}u(t)t^{-1/2}=0$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}M_{N}
=-1+\frac1{\log2}\int_{0}^{1}\frac{t^{-1/2}}{1+t}dt
=-1+\frac1{\log2}\,\frac{\pi}{2}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in units with the Pareto lower end at $1$

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}M_{N}= \frac{\pi}{2\log 2}-1\approx 1.27
\end{equation*}
above the lower end $x_{m}$.  In particular
${\mathbb E}[M_{N}-x_{m}]=\pi/(2\log2)-1$, so that with a
Pareto lower end $x_{m}>0$

\[
{\mathbb E}M_{N}=x_{m}\Bigl[\frac{\pi}{2\log 2}-1\Bigr]+x_{m}
      =x_{m}\frac{\pi}{2\log 2}\approx 2.27\,x_{m},
\]

the $+x_{m}$ being the usual shift $M_{N}=x_{m}\tilde M_{N}$.
On the conventional normalisation $x_{m}=1$ this is often quoted as
${\mathbb E}M_{N}\simeq\pi/(2\log2)\approx2.27$, the $1$ being the
Pareto lower cut-off.  The finite value is the consequence of the
random, log-series, monitoring horizon; conditionally on a fixed
$n$, ${\mathbb E}M_{n}\to\infty$ as $n\to\infty$, as it must for a
Pareto tail.
